docs/infra.md:82:The account's Workers Logs are rate limited: `GET /accounts/{id}/observability/usage/limit` (`cf observability usage-limit get`) reports `status: "limited"` and `sample_rate: 0.01`, so Cloudflare keeps a 1% head-based sample of every Worker's events (the documented behaviour once an account passes its daily limit; `resets_at` is null, so do not count on it ending). A single request has a 99% chance of leaving no event. This is why the spike's Main Worker, with logs on and about 25 requests, never showed up.
docs/infra.md:107:| `pr-<n>`, personal, `test_*` | Renders the message and records it in the stage's D1 table `captured_email`. Sends nothing. | No sending subdomain, no `send_email` binding, no event subscription |
docs/infra.md:114:cf d1 query <databaseId> --sql "SELECT recipient, subject, text FROM captured_email ORDER BY captured_at DESC LIMIT 10"
docs/infra.md:119:**Send states.** `pending → sending → sent | failed(reason)`. A delivery takes a two-minute lease before calling the provider, and the call times out after 30 seconds. A clear rejection (`E_RATE_LIMIT_EXCEEDED` and other coded errors) returns the send to `pending` and the message is retried after 30 s, 60 s, and 120 s; the fourth rejection fails it. Permanent rejections (`E_RECIPIENT_SUPPRESSED`, sender and header errors) fail at once. An error without a code, a timeout, or a lease that expired with no outcome fails the send as `unknown`, and it is never resent automatically. A message the queue dead-letters fails its send too (`abandoned`, or `rejected` after earlier rejections).
